Picture \par
\par
Since opening in 1997, Shakespeare\rquote s Globe Theatre has established itself as one of the most popular tourist sites in the capital, very much part of the outdoor life of a London summer. From January 2014, however, the theatre company will be playing all year round, thanks to the opening of the Sam Wanamaker Playhouse, an indoor theatre replicating as far as possible the playing space known as the Blackfriars, where a number of dramas by Shakespeare and his contemporaries were staged. Situated alongside the Globe on London\rquote s Bankside, the Jacobean-style theatre realises the original vision of the Globe\rquote s founder, Sam Wanamaker, allowing playgoers to compare the experience of watching Shakespeare in the two major venues in which his company, the King\rquote s Men, performed. \par
\par
Original practices \par
\par
Theatres such as the Globe, the indoor Jacobean theatre in Staunton, Virginia, and the new Playhouse open opportunities for research in an academic area often referred to as \lquote original practices\rquote . This field of theatrical studies is not without its detractors and some Shakespeare and performance specialists regard projects such as the reconstructed Globe as no more than a whimsical architectural fiction. For them, the inescapable truth is that there are very few hard facts from which to proceed. Much of what we know about theatre at this time is gathered from the published texts of the plays themselves and even these need to be approached with a degree of caution; most were published some considerable time after the original performances and may well have been supplemented and altered to gratify the tastes of readers, rather than spectators. \par
\par
Yet laying such scepticism aside, the reconstructed Globe has given audiences some ground-breaking productions and an intriguing insight into what theatre in Shakespeare\rquote s day might have been like. For first-time visitors to the amphitheatre, the experience often proves quite a shock to the senses: the sight of fellow audience members in broad daylight, the occasional cooing of a pigeon treading the boards and, for those standing in the Yard, the feel of the afternoon sun or, more likely, the afternoon rain on their faces. \par
\par
Today\rquote s groundlings \par
\par
There are, of course, some obvious limitations to creating authentic theatrical conditions, as the drone of planes making their way to and from Heathrow makes all too clear. It might be possible to replicate a piece of costume or the look of a thatched roof, but it is a great deal more difficult to replicate an audience. Twenty-first-century groundlings tend to differ little in social status from those who look down at them from the middle and upper galleries, and everyone \f1\emdash  excepting young children \emdash  will probably be literate. And while the audience might contain men and women, old and young, standers and sitters, as it did in the time of Shakespeare, it is unlikely to include beggars, prostitutes and ladies who, when caught short, urinate into a receptacle tucked under their skirts. \par
\par
Moving indoors \par
\par
Catering for a rather narrower demographic than would have been found at the Globe, the Blackfriars Playhouse in the City of London provided a second performance space for Shakespeare\rquote s company, from around 1609. What might more accurately be described as the second Blackfriars (an earlier attempt to open the theatre had been blocked by objections raised by residents), was the first purpose-built indoor theatre in England. Termed a \lquote private\rquote  playhouse \emdash  as opposed to the \lquote public\rquote  Globe \emdash  its high ticket prices ensured that it would be an upmarket venue, a place where playgoers could parade their finery, untroubled by the changeability of the English weather. \par
\par
Moving performance indoors had a profound impact on plays, actors and audiences. Dramas were no longer played straight through, but were divided into five acts, the four intervals between them allowing for the candles that lit the stage to be trimmed. During these brief interludes, spectators could gaze at the opulent wall-hangings, become better acquainted with their fellow playgoers, and listen to music performed by the theatre\rquote s highly accomplished instrumentalists. \par
\par
A sense of space \par
\par
The stage was no longer surrounded by those too poor to sit; rather, the pit contained rows of benches, seating a somewhat wealthier clientele. Unlike the Globe, where the cheapest ticket guaranteed the closest spot to the stage (and still does), seating tariffs at the Blackfriars set a pricing pattern which still holds in theatres today: the best view of the actors cost serious money. \par
\par
Yet if the indoor players no longer had to contend with the rumbustious antics of those in the Yard, they did have to manoeuvre their way round the gallants seated on stools on the sides of the stage itself. That this new form of proximity to the audience also brought its hazards is suggested in the Prologue to The Devil is an Ass, a city comedy by Shakespeare\rquote s contemporary, Ben Jonson. Addressed directly to the \lquote grandees\rquote  gracing the stage, the Prologue rails at how they: \par
\par
\'85thrust and spurn, \par
And knock us o\rquote  the elbows and, and bid turn; \par
As, if, when we had spoke, we must be gone\'85 \par
\par
If Jonson\rquote s prologue is anything to go by, the Blackfriars stage did not afford much elbow room (estimates vary, but it is thought to have been about 20\endash 30 feet across and about 15\endash 16 feet deep) and, at approximately half the size of the Globe stage, was more suited to an elegant duel than a full-blown battle scene. \par
\par
Lighting \par
\par
Flaming candles and the natural light that streamed through the theatre windows were used to illuminate the stage, providing atmospheric effects impossible to achieve in the broad daylight of an open theatre. Still today, we put our faith in the flickering chiaroscuro of candlelight to take us out of the realms of the everyday, whether casting a romantic glow over a dinner for two or a Gothic eeriness over a Halloween party. While an early modern audience would have been less susceptible to the lure of candlelight (candles were, after all, their main source of artificial light), the impact it made in the intimate space of an indoor playhouse, as it fell on the sumptuous fabrics and jewels of actors and audience alike, must have made viewing a Blackfriars performance a very different experience from viewing one at the Globe. \par
\par
The Duchess of Malfi \par
\par
A good case in point is Webster\rquote s revenge tragedy, The Duchess of Malfi, which, as is announced on the title page of the first quarto edition (1623), was performed at both the Globe and the Blackfriars. In one particularly chilling scene (4.1), Duke Ferdinand, the Duchess\rquote s twin brother, enters his sister\rquote s chamber in total darkness and offers her what is actually a severed hand, but which she believes to be his own. Several lines of dialogue are spoken on stage between the hand being offered and the Duchess discovering it to be that of a dead man. During this time, an actor on the Globe stage would have to hold the grotesque object in full view of the audience, seeming not to notice that it is detached from the brother who has offered it. Any performer, however skilled, would find it hard to prevent this moment in the drama from tipping over into darkly comic farce, especially at the point where the Duchess tells her brother: \lquote You are very cold. I fear you are not well after your travel\rquote . \par
\par
Imagine, though, this same scene played at the Blackfriars on a cold winter\rquote s day in December. The performance would have started at around two o\rquote clock in the afternoon; by the time it reached the fourth act, the natural light that came in through the theatre windows would have dimmed, and the instruction to Bosola to \lquote Take hence the lights\rquote  would have prompted the extinguishing of the stage candles, leaving the audience, if not in pitch blackness, at least in a crepuscular gloom. The Duchess\rquote s discovery of the dismembered hand when the lights are restored would be shared by the audience, creating an altogether more shocking impact than when played at the Globe. Perhaps this is why The Duchess of Malfi has been chosen to open the new Playhouse. \par
\par
Stage equipment \par
\par
If lighting effects afforded Blackfriars audiences gruesome thrills impossible to pull off in the full daylight of an amphitheatre, so too did the theatre\rquote s state-of-the-art stage equipment. Critics who argue that Shakespeare\rquote s late plays were written with the more high-tech facilities of the Blackfriars in mind (and there is plenty of scholarly disagreement in this area), often point to their extravagant visual effects as evidence: Jupiter descending on an eagle in Cymbeline, a statue coming alive in The Winter\rquote s Tale, a disappearing banquet in The Tempest. \par
\par
The Tempest \par
\par
Yet Andrew Gurr, a leading expert in early modern theatrical practices and academic advisor to Shakespeare\rquote s Globe, contends that The Tempest is the only one of Shakespeare\rquote s plays to be written specifically for the Blackfriars. The end of the fourth act has Prospero and Ariel exiting the stage, only to re-enter at the start of the final act, with Prospero donning his magic robe. Though such a stage action could conceivably have been incorporated into the type of continuous performance usual at the Globe, the re-entry of Prospero, splendidly attired in the cloak of the magus, would create an altogether more arresting theatrical moment following the musical interlude that punctuated indoor performances. \par
\par
Another reason to suggest that The Tempest was written specifically for the indoor theatre is that it is the most musical of all of Shakespeare\rquote s plays, designed to take full advantage of the superb singers and musicians employed at the Blackfriars. Many school students, in the process of performing a Shakespeare part in class, have encountered a shift from blank verse to song lyric and found themselves muttering \lquote Do I have to sing it?\rquote  \par
\par
The Tempest proves especially unnerving in this respect, possessing as it does the highest song-count of any Shakespeare play. Prospero\rquote s magical powers and his control of the island and its inhabitants are frequently manifested through Ariel\rquote s songs and the playing of instruments. Music places Ferdinand into position for his first sight of Miranda, works like an opiate to make the nobles sleep, and leads Caliban and his crew into a stinking pool. \par
\par
The magical island of Shakespeare\rquote s literary imagination is conjured up by the play\rquote s rich and varied soundscape: Stephano\rquote s drunken singing, the soft strains accompanying the betrothal masque, the solemn music providing the background for Prospero\rquote s ritualistic breaking of his staff. If indoor theatre was largely responsible for shifting the focus from \lquote hearing\rquote  a play to \lquote seeing\rquote  one, a Blackfriars performance of The Tempest, with its superior musical and visual effects, would have ensured a more or less even balance between the two. \par
\par
The new experience \par
\par
The new Playhouse has grown out of an original sketch described by the architects of the project as \lquote not in fact buildable\rquote  and has been developed through a combination of expert research, not a little guesswork and a great deal of passion. With a capacity of around 340, today\rquote s audiences will not experience quite the same degree of intimacy as the estimated 600 or so attending in the 1700s; nor will they be subjected to the thick smog of tobacco smoke that would have been a characteristic feature of a Blackfriars performance. They will, though, be offered the chance to see some of the finest Jacobean dramas performed in an atmosphere that is sure to prove stimulating for directors and unforgettable for audiences, even if questions about its authenticity remain tantalisingly unanswered. \par
\par
